## Android: keeping playback alive (foreground service + MediaSession)

### Why foreground services are critical on Android

Android can kill background-started processes quickly (especially after alarms, or when Android Auto starts the app) unless the app is in a foreground service state with a notification.

Bible Alarm uses one Android media session for the in-app player, the system notification, and Android Auto.

### One MediaLibraryService

Service:
- `src/Bible.Alarm/Platforms/Android/Services/Media3/BibleAlarmMediaLibraryService.cs`

It is a Media3 `MediaLibraryService`. The merged manifest declares both
`androidx.media3.session.MediaLibraryService` and `android.media.browse.MediaBrowserService`
on that one service. It does not declare `androidx.media3.session.MediaSessionService`
and it does not declare `androidx.car.app.CarAppService`. `automotive_app_desc.xml`
still uses `<uses name="media" />`, and the manifest keeps
`com.google.android.gms.car.application`.

Check after a Release Android build:

`pwsh tests/scripts/assert-android-manifest.ps1 -Path src/Bible.Alarm/obj/Release/net10.0-android/AndroidManifest.xml -ExpectedBrowseServices 1 -ExpectedMediaSessionServices 0`

The service owns the media notification through `DefaultMediaNotificationProvider`.
ExoPlayer does not create its own `MediaSession` and does not start a second
notification service. The player stays in
`AndroidGlobalExoPlayerFactory` (plain `ExoPlayerBuilder`,
`SetMaxSeekToPreviousPositionMs(long.MaxValue)`, no `SetAudioAttributes(..., true)`).

### Proxy player and the seam

`SessionStatePlayer` (`SimpleBasePlayer`) is the process-wide player the session
exposes. It does not own ExoPlayer. `Media3MediaSessionManager` implements
`IMediaSessionManager` and writes playback state and metadata into the proxy.
`MediaSessionEffect` is the Fluxor effect that calls that interface. Commands
from the notification or the car (`play`, `pause`, `next`, `previous`, seek)
go through `SessionCommandRouter` and the same messenger messages the in-app
player already handles.

Early process start (`MainApplication`, `AlarmSetupService`, `RestartReceiver`,
`AlarmRingerReceiver`) touches `SessionStatePlayer.Instance` before DI exists.

### Alarm handoff

`ForegroundServiceCoordinator` only keeps the process alive across alarm bootstrap.

1. `AlarmRingerReceiver` touches `SessionStatePlayer.Instance`, then calls
   `OnAlarmTriggered`.
2. `AlarmForegroundService` calls `StartForeground` immediately (notification id 2,
   "Preparing playback...").
3. When playback reaches `Playing`, `OnPlaybackStarted` starts
   `BibleAlarmMediaLibraryService` if it is not running.
4. The library service calls `StartForeground` with notification id 3, then
   `ReleaseAlarmIfPlaybackRunning` stops `AlarmForegroundService` only when this
   service is already in the foreground and playback is preparing or playing.
5. There is no timer that stops the alarm service. If Media3 reports
   `OnForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException`, the alarm service stays up and
   the listener posts a tap-to-resume notification.

Logcat tag is `BibleAlarm` (`adb logcat -s BibleAlarm`). The pair to watch is
`Alarm triggered (schedule {ScheduleId})` and
`Playback started. BibleAlarmMediaLibraryService owns the media notification.`
There is no Crashlytics, Sentry, or other production telemetry sink in the app.
A staged Play rollout has to use these log lines, crash rate, and the device
checklist in `docs/playback/device-checklist.md`. That checklist's device matrix
(Android 12, 14, 16, and a real head unit) has not been run for this cutover.

